### List all users
GET http://localhost:8002/users

### Filter users by role (query string)
GET http://localhost:8002/users?role=admin

### Get a single user by :id
GET http://localhost:8002/users/1

### /users/me — literal route registered before /users/:id, so it wins
### instead of being matched as :id="me" (which would 404 — no user has
### id "me"). Proves registration order, not just that a response comes back.
GET http://localhost:8002/users/me

### Create a user — creates Dana as id "4" (used by the DELETE demo below)
POST http://localhost:8002/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "name": "Dana",
    "role": "user"
}

### PUT fully replaces a user — every field comes from the body
PUT http://localhost:8002/users/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "name": "Alice Updated",
    "role": "admin"
}

### PUT on a nonexistent id — 404 via app logic, not routing
PUT http://localhost:8002/users/999
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "name": "Nobody",
    "role": "user"
}

### PATCH partially updates a user — only the given fields change
PATCH http://localhost:8002/users/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "role": "user"
}

### List a user's posts
GET http://localhost:8002/users/1/posts

### Get one specific post — multi-param route, both :userId and :postId
GET http://localhost:8002/users/1/posts/101

### Wrong postId for a real user — 404 via app logic, not routing
GET http://localhost:8002/users/1/posts/999

### Unmatched path — no route registered for this at all, plain-text 404
GET http://localhost:8002/nonexistent

### HEAD auto-dispatches to the matching GET handler, body discarded,
### headers (Content-Type/Content-Length) still set as GET would
HEAD http://localhost:8002/users/1

### OPTIONS on a real path — no app.options() registered here, so this is
### the automatic response: 204 with an Allow header listing every method
### actually available at /users/1 (GET, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, plus the
### auto-added HEAD and OPTIONS itself)
OPTIONS http://localhost:8002/users/1

### OPTIONS on a completely unregistered path — still 404, same as any
### other verb; there's no server-wide "OPTIONS *" special-casing
OPTIONS http://localhost:8002/nonexistent

### POST to /users/me — 405, not 404 (the path matches, just not for this
### method). Allow lists GET, HEAD, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, OPTIONS — not just
### GET/HEAD from the literal /users/me route. /users/:id structurally
### matches "/users/me" too (id="me"), and 405's Allow computation
### considers every pattern that matches the path, not just whichever
### route would actually have been selected — so PUT/PATCH/DELETE from the
### colliding :id route show up here as well.
POST http://localhost:8002/users/me

### DELETE removes Dana (created above as id "4") — 204, no body
DELETE http://localhost:8002/users/4

### DELETE on an id that never existed — 404 via app logic, not routing
DELETE http://localhost:8002/users/999
